UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 1: Animal features and adaptations', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours).","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nMatch feature→help: thick fur; long beak for nectar; sharp claws for climbing. Invent one feature for a desert animal and say how it helps.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFor eight animals, record one adaptation and how it helps survival. Design a labelled “invented animal” for a named habitat with three justified features.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours).","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: For eight animals, record one adaptation and how it helps survival. Design a labelled “invented animal” for a named habitat with three justified features. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food. Reasoning: I used adaptation by attending to feature and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. Correction: Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food. Why: the correction restores accurate use of adaptation.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses adaptation. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about adaptation, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link. / Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food. This matches the lesson skill for adaptation.","options":["Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food."],"question":"Match feature→help: thick fur; long beak for nectar; sharp claws for climbing. Invent one feature for a desert animal and say how it helps. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food.","Careful use of feature and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete.","Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours)."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named feature and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the feature and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising adaptation. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence.","options":["Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete.","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone.","Decide from which object looks most exciting.","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation."],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, glass marble, fallen dry leaf. Which habit makes a scientific sort or claim trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim.","options":["Named observation plus how it supports the claim.","A longer story with no observation.","A label copied from the title with no evidence.","Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete."],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food. applies the science idea with evidence.","options":["Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food.","Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete."],"question":"Using this idea — Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or… — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable and control the rest.","options":["Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same.","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster.","Stop early when you like the first result.","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer."],"question":"Which investigation habit keeps a comparison fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Transfer needs a new object or context with evidence.","options":["Test or describe a new object or context and cite fresh evidence.","Repeat the worked example objects unchanged.","Invent a dramatic result with no observation.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work."],"question":"Which next step best shows transfer of adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses adaptation. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food. Reasoning: I used adaptation by attending to feature and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason.","question":"Name one science misconception about adaptation, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. Correction: Fur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food. Why: the correction restores accurate use of adaptation.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w03-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise adaptation.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Living World","success_criteria":["I can use adaptation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify feature and explain why it matters.","I can practise survive independently on a new prompt.","I can notice environment needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours). Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours)."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA duck’s webbed feet help swimming. A cactus stores water in dry places. Nocturnal animals feed at night when it is cooler or safer.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMatch feature→help: thick fur; long beak for nectar; sharp claws for climbing. Invent one feature for a desert animal and say how it helps.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food.","explain":"Adaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Adaptation meaning.","caption":"Link feature to how it helps.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Adaptation</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">feature that helps survival</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Example</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">webbed feet → swim</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A duck’s webbed feet help swimming. A cactus stores water in dry places. Nocturnal animals feed at night when it is cool Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A duck’s webbed feet help swimming. A cactus stores water in dry places. Nocturnal animals feed at night when it is cool Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA duck’s webbed feet help swimming. A cactus stores water in dry places. Nocturnal animals feed at night when it is cooler or safer. Look at each observation, variable or design choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Features match environments.","caption":"Pretty alone is incomplete.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Desert</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">store water / night activity</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Habitat link</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">feature fits place</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. Here is a more productive model: A duck’s webbed feet help swimming. A cactus stores water in dry places. Nocturnal animals feed at night when it is cooler or safer.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFur→warmth; beak→reach flowers; claws→grip. Desert idea must link to heat/water/food.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Calling every pretty colour an adaptation without saying how it helps is incomplete. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about adaptation?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAdaptations are features that help a living thing survive in its environment (body parts or behaviours). Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvestigate or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different objects or data.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of observations.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 1: Animal features and adaptations","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Living things and habitats","week":3,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA duck’s webbed feet help swimming. A cactus stores water in dry places. Nocturnal animals feed at night when it is cooler or safer.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Adaptation meaning.","caption":"Link feature to how it helps.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Adaptation</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">feature that helps survival</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Example</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">webbed feet → swim</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Features match environments.","caption":"Pretty alone is incomplete.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Desert</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">store water / night activity</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Habitat link</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">feature fits place</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-sci-y3-w03-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
